WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.959
Welcome back.

00:00:00.999 --> 00:00:06.647
In this episode we answer a question
that sits underneath the whole topic:

00:00:06.687 --> 00:00:11.299
when a fuel burns, where does
the energy actually come from?

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:15.151
First, a quick retrieval
check from last time.

00:00:15.191 --> 00:00:19.517
Hydrogen burns: two hydrogen
plus oxygen gives two water.

00:00:19.557 --> 00:00:24.497
If zero point five zero zero moles
of hydrogen reacts completely,

00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:26.565
what amount of water forms?

00:00:26.605 --> 00:00:28.291
Pause and work it out.

00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:42.473
The ratio of hydrogen to water is
two to two, which is one to one.

00:00:42.513 --> 00:00:46.852
So zero point five zero
zero moles of water forms.

00:00:46.892 --> 00:00:52.584
The coefficients gave us a mole
ratio, exactly as in episode one.

00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:54.588
Now the big question.

00:00:54.628 --> 00:01:01.123
A common belief is that energy is stored
inside chemical bonds and comes out when

00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:02.414
the bonds break.

00:01:02.454 --> 00:01:06.529
It sounds reasonable, but
it's the wrong way round.

00:01:06.569 --> 00:01:11.128
By the end of this episode
you'll be able to explain why,

00:01:11.168 --> 00:01:16.412
and to calculate an estimate of
the energy change for a reaction.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.919
To talk about energy changes
precisely, we split the world in two.

00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:25.235
The system is the reacting
chemicals themselves,

00:01:25.275 --> 00:01:27.999
the substances whose bonds are changing.

00:01:28.039 --> 00:01:33.318
Everything else is the surroundings:
the water they're dissolved in, the cup,

00:01:33.358 --> 00:01:35.085
the thermometer, the air.

00:01:35.466 --> 00:01:41.396
When a reaction happens, energy can be
transferred across the boundary between the

00:01:41.436 --> 00:01:43.434
system and the surroundings.

00:01:43.474 --> 00:01:48.530
Energy transferred because of a
temperature difference is called heat.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.805
So heat is not a substance
that flows around.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:54.393
It's a name for energy on the move.

00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:56.359
Temperature is different.

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:59.157
Temperature measures how hot something is,

00:01:59.197 --> 00:02:03.088
which reflects the average
kinetic energy of its particles.

00:02:03.128 --> 00:02:06.859
It is not the total amount of
energy something contains.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.562
Here's an example.

00:02:08.602 --> 00:02:14.017
A cup of tea at eighty degrees is
hotter than a bath at forty degrees.

00:02:14.057 --> 00:02:19.783
But the bath contains far more water,
so it holds far more thermal energy.

00:02:19.823 --> 00:02:23.289
Higher temperature does
not mean more energy.

00:02:23.329 --> 00:02:29.056
In calorimetry we'll use temperature
changes to work out how much heat was

00:02:29.096 --> 00:02:30.031
transferred.

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:35.922
In an exothermic reaction, energy is
transferred from the system to the

00:02:35.962 --> 00:02:36.831
surroundings.

00:02:36.871 --> 00:02:39.207
The surroundings gain that energy,

00:02:39.247 --> 00:02:43.122
so the water warms up and the
thermometer reading rises.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:45.608
Combustion of a fuel is exothermic.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:51.446
In an endothermic reaction, energy is
transferred from the surroundings into the

00:02:51.486 --> 00:02:51.926
system.

00:02:51.966 --> 00:02:57.619
The surroundings lose energy, so the water
cools and the thermometer reading falls.

00:02:57.659 --> 00:03:00.608
An instant cold pack
is a familiar example.

00:03:00.999 --> 00:03:03.832
We can attach signs to these transfers.

00:03:03.872 --> 00:03:08.916
Energy gained counts as positive,
and energy lost counts as negative.

00:03:08.956 --> 00:03:15.030
In an exothermic reaction the system loses
energy, so q for the system is negative,

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.091
while q for the surroundings is positive.

00:03:18.466 --> 00:03:20.848
If no energy escapes anywhere else,

00:03:20.888 --> 00:03:24.239
these two are equal in
size and opposite in sign.

00:03:24.279 --> 00:03:27.630
Whatever the system loses,
the surroundings gain.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.297
That single idea, energy is
conserved and only moves,

00:03:31.337 --> 00:03:35.144
underpins every calorimetry
calculation in this series.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:38.810
So where does the energy come from?

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.762
Start with a single hydrogen molecule.

00:03:41.802 --> 00:03:45.024
The two atoms are held together by a bond,

00:03:45.064 --> 00:03:49.528
an attraction between the nuclei
and the shared electrons.

00:03:49.568 --> 00:03:53.489
To pull the atoms apart,
you have to supply energy.

00:03:53.529 --> 00:03:56.480
Breaking a bond always absorbs energy.

00:03:56.865 --> 00:03:58.646
Now run it the other way.

00:03:58.686 --> 00:04:03.233
When two separate hydrogen atoms
come together and form a bond,

00:04:03.273 --> 00:04:06.437
they settle into a
lower-energy arrangement,

00:04:06.477 --> 00:04:09.568
and energy is released
to the surroundings.

00:04:09.608 --> 00:04:12.302
Making a bond always releases energy.

00:04:12.665 --> 00:04:15.625
Think of two strong
magnets stuck together.

00:04:15.665 --> 00:04:19.393
Pulling them apart takes
effort: that's bond breaking.

00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:23.858
Letting them snap together gives
energy out: that's bond making.

00:04:23.898 --> 00:04:28.642
Neither magnet contains energy that
pops out when you separate them.

00:04:28.999 --> 00:04:32.972
So a reaction's overall
energy change is a balance.

00:04:33.012 --> 00:04:36.984
Energy goes in to break
the bonds in the reactants,

00:04:37.024 --> 00:04:41.469
and energy comes out when the
bonds in the products form.

00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:47.332
If more energy comes out than went in,
the reaction is exothermic overall.

00:04:48.265 --> 00:04:52.567
Practice question Q zero
three is worth four marks.

00:04:52.607 --> 00:04:55.246
Read it, then pause and try it.

00:05:07.665 --> 00:05:12.408
We're asked for the enthalpy change
for the equation as written,

00:05:12.448 --> 00:05:18.611
so for one mole of hydrogen reacting with
one mole of chlorine to form two moles of

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:19.956
hydrogen chloride.

00:05:19.996 --> 00:05:24.290
The model is: energy absorbed
to break all reactant bonds,

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:28.067
minus energy released when
all product bonds form.

00:05:28.465 --> 00:05:30.765
Start the ledger with bonds broken.

00:05:30.805 --> 00:05:36.246
One hydrogen to hydrogen bond, at four
hundred and thirty-six kilojoules per mole,

00:05:36.286 --> 00:05:40.658
and one chlorine to chlorine bond,
at two hundred and forty-three.

00:05:40.698 --> 00:05:44.909
Total energy absorbed: six hundred
and seventy-nine kilojoules.

00:05:45.265 --> 00:05:46.369
Now bonds formed.

00:05:46.409 --> 00:05:49.734
The equation makes two
moles of hydrogen chloride,

00:05:49.774 --> 00:05:55.321
so two hydrogen to chlorine bonds form,
each releasing four hundred and thirty-one.

00:05:55.361 --> 00:05:59.493
Total energy released: eight
hundred and sixty-two kilojoules.

00:05:59.533 --> 00:06:01.889
Don't miss that coefficient of two.

00:06:02.265 --> 00:06:04.640
Before subtracting, predict the sign.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:09.600
More energy is released than absorbed,
so the reaction should be exothermic,

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.728
with a negative enthalpy change.

00:06:12.065 --> 00:06:15.768
Enthalpy change equals energy
absorbed minus energy released:

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:19.635
six hundred and seventy-nine
minus eight hundred and sixty-two,

00:06:19.675 --> 00:06:24.360
which is negative one hundred and
eighty-three kilojoules for the equation as

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:24.851
written.

00:06:24.891 --> 00:06:29.984
It's an estimate, because average bond
enthalpies are averages over many compounds.

00:06:30.332 --> 00:06:33.568
For the explanation mark,
link cause and effect:

00:06:33.608 --> 00:06:39.234
the reaction is exothermic because more
energy is released forming the two hydrogen

00:06:39.274 --> 00:06:44.763
to chlorine bonds than is absorbed breaking
the hydrogen to hydrogen and chlorine

00:06:44.803 --> 00:06:45.992
to chlorine bonds.

00:06:46.032 --> 00:06:51.561
Simply saying "bonds are formed" misses
the comparison, which is the whole point.

00:06:51.932 --> 00:06:56.327
Marks: one for the broken total,
one for the formed total,

00:06:56.367 --> 00:07:02.637
one for the subtraction with the correct
sign, and one for the causal explanation.

00:07:02.998 --> 00:07:04.382
Two tempting errors.

00:07:04.422 --> 00:07:10.076
Adding every bond energy together gives
a large positive number with no physical

00:07:10.116 --> 00:07:10.645
meaning.

00:07:10.685 --> 00:07:15.058
And treating bond breaking as
the release step flips the sign,

00:07:15.098 --> 00:07:18.260
giving positive one
hundred and eighty-three.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:23.994
In both cases, the first wrong step is
not separating energy in from energy out.

00:07:24.332 --> 00:07:25.546
Changed condition.

00:07:25.586 --> 00:07:28.125
Suppose the product bond were weaker,

00:07:28.165 --> 00:07:33.700
so that forming the two new bonds released
only six hundred kilojoules in total.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.458
Would the reaction still be exothermic?

00:07:42.865 --> 00:07:43.026
No.

00:07:43.066 --> 00:07:48.309
Six hundred and seventy-nine absorbed
minus six hundred released gives positive

00:07:48.349 --> 00:07:51.787
seventy-nine kilojoules, so
it would be endothermic.

00:07:51.827 --> 00:07:55.866
The sign depends on the balance
between breaking and forming,

00:07:55.906 --> 00:07:58.715
not on bonds being broken or formed alone.

00:07:59.664 --> 00:08:05.230
Chemists summarise all of this with a
quantity called enthalpy, written H.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:10.230
You can think of it as the total
chemical energy, or heat content,

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:12.730
of a system at constant pressure.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.997
We can't measure absolute enthalpy,
but we can measure changes in it.

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:23.499
The enthalpy change, delta H, equals
the enthalpy of the products minus the

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:25.305
enthalpy of the reactants.

00:08:25.345 --> 00:08:29.818
On a diagram, reactants sit at
one level and products at another.

00:08:29.858 --> 00:08:32.249
For the hydrogen chloride reaction,

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:36.664
the products sit one hundred and
eighty-three kilojoules lower.

00:08:36.997 --> 00:08:40.821
Because the products are
lower, delta H is negative.

00:08:40.861 --> 00:08:43.793
The system has less enthalpy at the end,

00:08:43.833 --> 00:08:48.772
and the difference was transferred
out to the surroundings as heat.

00:08:48.812 --> 00:08:51.447
A negative delta H means exothermic.

00:08:51.487 --> 00:08:57.579
A positive delta H means the products sit
higher, and the reaction is endothermic.

00:08:57.931 --> 00:08:58.973
Now the language.

00:08:59.013 --> 00:09:03.750
Delta H equals negative one hundred
and eighty-three kilojoules is a signed

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:05.724
quantity describing the system.

00:09:05.764 --> 00:09:08.208
The same fact, said as energy released,

00:09:08.248 --> 00:09:12.729
is one hundred and eighty-three
kilojoules released, a positive amount.

00:09:12.769 --> 00:09:17.378
Avoid saying "negative one hundred
and eighty-three kilojoules released".

00:09:17.418 --> 00:09:22.003
That's a double negative, which would
actually mean energy was absorbed.

00:09:22.397 --> 00:09:23.079
Checkpoint.

00:09:23.119 --> 00:09:27.147
One student writes "delta H equals
negative fifty kilojoules".

00:09:27.187 --> 00:09:30.821
Another writes "fifty kilojoules
of energy is released".

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:33.314
Are they describing the same reaction?

00:09:33.354 --> 00:09:34.470
Pause and decide.

00:09:42.931 --> 00:09:43.192
Yes.

00:09:43.232 --> 00:09:49.288
Both describe an exothermic change in
which fifty kilojoules of energy leaves the

00:09:49.328 --> 00:09:49.814
system.

00:09:49.854 --> 00:09:52.373
One uses a signed enthalpy change,

00:09:52.413 --> 00:09:56.889
and the other uses the positive
size of the energy released.

00:09:56.929 --> 00:10:00.616
Both are correct if each
one is labelled clearly.

00:10:01.594 --> 00:10:05.566
Practice question Q zero
four is worth three marks.

00:10:05.606 --> 00:10:08.989
Pause the video and
answer all three parts.

00:10:21.394 --> 00:10:26.655
The dissolution absorbs energy, so energy
is transferred from the calorimeter into

00:10:26.695 --> 00:10:28.077
the dissolving system.

00:10:28.117 --> 00:10:30.444
Every arrow points inward this time.

00:10:30.794 --> 00:10:36.134
The system's energy change is the energy
absorbed per mole times the amount:

00:10:36.174 --> 00:10:41.938
eighteen point zero kilojoules per mole
times zero point zero two five zero moles,

00:10:41.978 --> 00:10:45.831
which is positive zero point
four five zero kilojoules.

00:10:45.871 --> 00:10:49.128
It's positive because
the system gains energy.

00:10:49.461 --> 00:10:51.785
The calorimeter supplies that energy,

00:10:51.825 --> 00:10:55.811
so its change is negative zero
point four five zero kilojoules.

00:10:55.851 --> 00:11:00.580
And because the calorimeter's contents
lose energy, the temperature falls.

00:11:00.928 --> 00:11:05.126
One mark each: the system's
change with its positive sign,

00:11:05.166 --> 00:11:10.754
the calorimeter's change with its negative
sign, and the falling temperature.

00:11:10.794 --> 00:11:14.262
The tempting error is
giving both the same sign.

00:11:14.302 --> 00:11:18.613
Ask yourself who gains and who
loses, and the signs follow.

00:11:18.994 --> 00:11:22.648
Notice that endothermic
doesn't mean energy disappears.

00:11:22.688 --> 00:11:26.812
Every kilojoule the system absorbs
came from the surroundings.

00:11:26.852 --> 00:11:31.579
If twice as much solute dissolved,
twice as much energy would transfer,

00:11:31.619 --> 00:11:34.937
and the temperature would
fall about twice as far,

00:11:34.977 --> 00:11:37.328
if the calorimeter stayed the same.

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:42.113
Combustion has one more
bond to remember: oxygen's.

00:11:42.153 --> 00:11:48.371
When methane burns, the ledger breaks four
carbon to hydrogen bonds and two oxygen

00:11:48.411 --> 00:11:50.126
to oxygen double bonds.

00:11:50.166 --> 00:11:56.308
Then it forms two carbon to oxygen double
bonds in carbon dioxide and four oxygen

00:11:56.348 --> 00:11:58.368
to hydrogen bonds in water.

00:11:58.408 --> 00:12:01.919
Leaving out the oxygen
bonds is a common slip.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:08.132
Combustion releases so much energy largely
because the bonds in carbon dioxide and

00:12:08.172 --> 00:12:12.785
water are very strong, so forming
them releases a lot of energy.

00:12:13.127 --> 00:12:17.360
Now compare three carbon compounds
on a ladder of oxidation.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.208
Methane's carbon has no oxygen attached.

00:12:20.248 --> 00:12:24.338
In methanol, one oxygen is
already attached to the carbon.

00:12:24.378 --> 00:12:28.611
Carbon dioxide is fully oxidised
and can't burn any further.

00:12:28.651 --> 00:12:33.880
In oxidation numbers, the carbon goes
from negative four, to negative two,

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:35.131
to positive four.

00:12:35.527 --> 00:12:39.608
A fuel that is already partly
oxidised has, in a sense,

00:12:39.648 --> 00:12:42.306
already done some of its combustion.

00:12:42.346 --> 00:12:45.827
So burning it has less
further oxidation to do,

00:12:45.867 --> 00:12:48.675
and that tends to release less energy.

00:12:48.715 --> 00:12:54.444
The data fit this idea: one mole of
methane releases eight hundred and ninety

00:12:54.484 --> 00:12:59.015
kilojoules, while one mole of
methanol, also with one carbon,

00:12:59.055 --> 00:13:01.902
releases seven hundred and twenty-six.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:06.169
But treat this as a qualitative
idea, not a shortcut.

00:13:06.209 --> 00:13:12.202
An exact comparison needs the full balanced
reaction, the states of the products,

00:13:12.242 --> 00:13:16.150
and a consistent basis, such
as per mole or per gram.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.766
We'll compare fuels
carefully in later episodes.

00:13:20.727 --> 00:13:21.493
Let's recap.

00:13:21.533 --> 00:13:26.533
The system is the reacting chemicals;
the surroundings are everything else.

00:13:26.573 --> 00:13:31.505
Heat is energy transferred, and
temperature measures how hot something is.

00:13:31.545 --> 00:13:35.536
Breaking bonds absorbs energy,
and making bonds releases it.

00:13:35.576 --> 00:13:37.955
Delta H is products minus reactants,

00:13:37.995 --> 00:13:42.497
and a negative value means energy
was released to the surroundings.

00:13:42.860 --> 00:13:43.857
Closing recall.

00:13:43.897 --> 00:13:48.003
In one sentence, explain why
burning a fuel releases energy,

00:13:48.043 --> 00:13:50.669
using the words "broken" and "formed".

00:14:01.127 --> 00:14:06.994
Here's a strong answer: more energy is
released when the bonds in the products are

00:14:07.034 --> 00:14:11.820
formed than is absorbed when the
bonds in the reactants are broken.

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:17.583
Next episode, we'll draw these changes as
energy profiles and learn how to scale

00:14:17.623 --> 00:14:20.288
and reverse thermochemical equations.
